#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb99e6f5-005f-543f-b6c9-a0dc1c304d99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a2057fd-fafc-5925-b237-102e4ef70503" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381097} {:answer/id #uuid "c1a94faa-ef95-51eb-b9b6-a36d71297684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381098}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1a94faa-ef95-51eb-b9b6-a36d71297684" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381098} :answer-field/id #uuid "4673e41b-2429-50a1-b9b4-22206955e8a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3c294091-0460-52e0-836a-793055353031" :question/math-academy-id "q-330948" :question/problem "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(3t+4)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(3t+4)^{2}=9{t}^{2}+24t+16\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{9{t}^{2}+24t+16} \\\\  & =L{9{t}^{2}}+L{24t}+L{16} \\\\  & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+24s+16{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(8{s}^{2}+12s+9)}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e446462f-5423-5004-b0c3-12566bdb6eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381101} {:answer/id #uuid "1efef38f-fe16-5ef1-957f-39dc877af1cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381102} {:answer/id #uuid "28d2b936-e050-57b9-8204-c393a10ba8d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381103} {:answer/id #uuid "c6818764-ab64-502e-9553-05d0e9a4726e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381104} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c63880-b7b2-5eaf-8275-bd7a4773abc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e446462f-5423-5004-b0c3-12566bdb6eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381101} :answer-field/id #uuid "52658998-d645-56f6-b5df-343b402efc53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08f4acd9-0162-5e07-b55e-d0e4a1671202" :question/math-academy-id "q-332674" :question/problem "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "32e63c33-8820-542f-afab-f27a1f6a847a" :knowledge-point/title "Proving the Smoothness Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a43dda4f-aa46-525c-b03f-485d6458d443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220612} {:answer/id #uuid "c30b7646-897f-5455-9161-e26a9046e747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220613} {:answer/id #uuid "5663368c-3b85-50ef-b92b-96274f65ec12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the continuity of partial derivatives" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c30b7646-897f-5455-9161-e26a9046e747" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220613} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf1f1b4-b06a-507a-84e7-80c87f923bf4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220611} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5040251-30be-5821-a978-d2e574fdaf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220616} {:answer/id #uuid "d0f7b1af-3202-5deb-8e00-1615f8d0248c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "differentiating under the integral sign" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220617} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b74fa7-7702-58a0-b129-e576c8b260e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220618} {:answer/id #uuid "464506da-d861-55af-89de-cab00b1f4dd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "integration by parts" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b5040251-30be-5821-a978-d2e574fdaf0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220616} :answer-field/id #uuid "6999a3d8-470e-55c6-bb84-2e9b47b0ae4d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220615} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3170702-c578-5f56-a779-195f44568d79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the linearity of the Laplace transform" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220621} {:answer/id #uuid "3c5b52eb-40f6-530f-8f3e-cf6fee8e8d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the product rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220622} {:answer/id #uuid "53dbbbf4-d538-5315-98b3-bf70b7ce09ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53dbbbf4-d538-5315-98b3-bf70b7ce09ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "the chain rule" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220623} :answer-field/id #uuid "133ad3e3-e7f1-5a96-a348-9e104655f6a7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220620}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40ff4752-2fb6-5924-9814-2ff991abb48a" :question/math-academy-id "q-334874" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221955 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6373d849-f5c6-50d2-ad4f-72f77a1a8c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (2nd Derivative)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afb4bb4e-ca87-5399-ad3b-19806dc8c12a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s^{2}F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220718} {:answer/id #uuid "f06b5537-2906-5526-a02d-8683db80bce4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− s^{2}F^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220719} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e3a225-b1fb-5ef3-85cd-2b7cf6fbe1d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220720} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff598f8-13ac-500b-9d94-0fd1e448679c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− s^{2}F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220721} {:answer/id #uuid "6da14cef-941b-5d56-9632-db5c4b6e5751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da14cef-941b-5d56-9632-db5c4b6e5751" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220722} :answer-field/id #uuid "973d2ff0-63b3-5366-b052-8d5dbeded7d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220717} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15793f75-6f65-5df9-ba30-67d5f7d92711" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2s}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381107} {:answer/id #uuid "42052ce3-ea4e-531d-8291-b2609d0ac921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381108} {:answer/id #uuid "5b955eef-7455-54f9-a851-8c764b5d814a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381109} {:answer/id #uuid "a469c26d-cf9c-55ab-a32f-2a9f2f2ce17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381110} {:answer/id #uuid "18cc850c-90e9-59d3-a2bd-2e37ad1045b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381111}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a469c26d-cf9c-55ab-a32f-2a9f2f2ce17a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381110} :answer-field/id #uuid "992b8799-4f9a-5ab7-b2d4-a9f80d62a058" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381106} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "664eb1b8-b64e-5200-a18b-4c1d56773185" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2s}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381113} {:answer/id #uuid "9b6c7c64-6929-556d-9d5d-c5c5f964c51c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381114} {:answer/id #uuid "4952224e-25ba-55a8-a225-5c07a5746a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381115} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9294d4-5887-5ecc-9130-fc50fdf6e7d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2s}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4952224e-25ba-55a8-a225-5c07a5746a03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381115} :answer-field/id #uuid "21f3ad76-b579-5693-bc4e-57d3a5110069" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3fd3086-f746-5979-a170-4c67285d4998" :question/math-academy-id "q-340844" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform $L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}$ using the smoothness property. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $f(t)={e}^{4t}$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={{field-3}},\\,s>4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, for $s>4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{s-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s-4)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s-4)^{-2} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(s-4)^{-2}) \\\\  & =2(s-4)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}},\\,s>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6373d849-f5c6-50d2-ad4f-72f77a1a8c92" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Laplace Transforms Using the Smoothness Property (2nd Derivative)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7800cd9-90dd-5697-921e-8dc13a9d50af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− s^{2}F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220730} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b6316e-d9ea-594f-aa2d-e4037c59c724" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− s^{2}F^{′}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220731} {:answer/id #uuid "df601bbd-b8ed-5ddc-8f7c-334bcbdcb89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220732} {:answer/id #uuid "13086723-af32-594d-92ef-66ed13ea8572" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "− F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220733} {:answer/id #uuid "276021ae-5f79-540b-81b6-173e3b1fab70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s^{2}F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df601bbd-b8ed-5ddc-8f7c-334bcbdcb89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "F^{″}(s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220732} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2afc6f7-0ec8-5d15-a024-4b5747a94919" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220729} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b590fc-5f86-52c6-bd92-5bc4f2b98b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381118} {:answer/id #uuid "ac66a03a-359a-5e37-a080-93c185cd8aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381119} {:answer/id #uuid "81da3657-863d-5f66-94c2-dfaa96b83067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381120} {:answer/id #uuid "16f270b0-e92d-5c1f-aaeb-7028cf5a1c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47b590fc-5f86-52c6-bd92-5bc4f2b98b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381118} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ad00210-691c-56eb-a6cd-4f290495347e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381117} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c0f9c8-68be-5dd2-b8e8-53472b722ea9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{2}-32}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381123} {:answer/id #uuid "080854da-e2f9-527e-84b6-8c8c531b3db2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381124} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd2d340-8520-563c-9330-692982d27fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16{s}^{2}-24}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381125} {:answer/id #uuid "d00bcdc4-6d4d-5af0-ab30-3b96d9e44cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d00bcdc4-6d4d-5af0-ab30-3b96d9e44cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381126} :answer-field/id #uuid "5127ddb6-0fa1-5832-9797-d20722f77f41" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "140cc551-bcdf-5485-bb12-a67ff7d370ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{24{s}^{3}-128s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381128} {:answer/id #uuid "2582560d-da54-5dea-a97c-a3041dc0419a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381129} {:answer/id #uuid "d537406d-c3ee-5d4b-bd54-2fc366d3a96a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{3}-128s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381130} {:answer/id #uuid "cd199073-2260-55d6-b52c-cf38cb326d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd199073-2260-55d6-b52c-cf38cb326d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381131} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7fbdc18-5153-522f-8eec-415d5c6ad820" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1112abd-9d76-5cb7-a3f0-3aa50fdea8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-340850" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform \n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below. You may make use of the following result:\n\n$$\nL{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\omega t}=\\frac{\\omega }{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nLet $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}={{field-2}},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={{field-4}},\\,s>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t.$ Using the given result with $\\omega =4,$ the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is \n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16},\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(4({s}^{2}+16)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-4({s}^{2}+16)^{-2}\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-\\frac{8s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-\\frac{8s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{(s)^{′}\\cdot ({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-s\\cdot {(({s}^{2}+16)^{2})}^{′}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-s\\cdot 2({s}^{2}+16)\\cdot 2s}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{({s}^{2}+16)^{2}-4{s}^{2}({s}^{2}+16)}{({s}^{2}+16)^{4}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{{s}^{2}+16-4{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =-8\\cdot (\\frac{16-3{s}^{2}}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the quotient rule to find ${F}^{″}(s).$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{24{s}^{2}-128}{({s}^{2}+16)^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
